narrative:
## The Uniqueness Container

A **Set** is like a Vec, but with one crucial difference: duplicates are silently ignored. Every element in a Set is unique.

### Creating a Set

```xiom
let tags: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
```

### Inserting Items

```xiom
tags.insert("important");
tags.insert("urgent");
tags.insert("important");
```

Even though we inserted "important" twice, the Set only keeps one copy. The second `.insert()` does nothing.

### Checking Membership: .contains()

```xiom
if tags.contains("important") {
  io.println("This item is marked important");
}
```

`.contains()` returns `Bool` — `true` if the element is in the Set, `false` if not.

### Removing Items: .remove()

```xiom
tags.remove("urgent");
```

`.remove()` returns `Bool` — `true` if the item was present and removed, `false` if it wasn't there.

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let permissions: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
  permissions.insert("read");
  permissions.insert("write");
  permissions.insert("read");

  io.println(permissions.contains("read"));
  io.println(permissions.contains("admin"));

  io.println(permissions.remove("write"));
  io.println(permissions.contains("write"));

  return 0;
}
```

### Set vs Vec vs Map

| Collection | Use When |
|-----------|---------|
| Vec | Ordered, duplicates OK, access by index |
| Map | Key-value pairs, fast lookup by key |
| Set | Uniqueness matters, membership testing |

